5. Limitations

Haar cascades are fast but less accurate than modern deep learning detectors (MTCNN, RetinaFace). They struggle with profile faces, occlusions, and extreme lighting.
FER library was trained on FER‑2013, which contains label noise and class imbalance. Performance degrades on unconstrained, real‑world images.
Frame skipping can cause emotion “lag” when the face changes expression rapidly.
Multiple faces – the current visualisation may overlap bar panels; the system still processes all detected faces.
6. Future Work

Replace Haar cascades with MTCNN or a lightweight CNN face detector for higher accuracy.
Fine‑tune the emotion model on a custom dataset using TensorFlow/Keras.
Add face tracking (e.g., with OpenCV’s tracker API) to maintain per‑face emotion history.
Implement emotion logging – record emotions over time to a CSV file.
Build a GUI (Tkinter/PyQt) for easier configuration and live plotting.
7. Conclusion

This project delivers a functional, modular, and configurable facial emotion recognition system. It works out‑of‑the‑box with a webcam or on a static dataset, provides informative visualisations, and allows quantitative assessment through batch evaluation. The code is suitable for educational demonstrations, prototyping, and as a foundation for further research.

